<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="JMT-MS-15 - Story LF-338 Simultaneous Admin Decision Race">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Story LF-338, the one thing about the admin decision rule xUnit cannot prove: two REAL, SIMULTANEOUS HTTP requests deciding the SAME appeal, which is what happens whenever an appeal is genuinely ambiguous and a second admin has the queue open in another tab. AdminAppealsApiIntegrationTests covers the sequential case and asserts that at most one match row results; this plan proves the concurrent case, where the second decision has to lose on a re-read of the row&apos;s status rather than after a stale in-memory check.

Exactly 2 threads, both authenticated as the same admin (ADMIN_TOKEN), both targeting the identical pending appeal from RACE_APPEAL_CSV. There is no preview step - the decision endpoint does not take a previewVersion - so the only thing arranging the collision is the Synchronizing Timer, which holds both threads until both are ready and then releases them together.

Verify is raced rather than verify-against-reject on purpose. Verify is the outcome with permanent consequences: it CREATES A MATCH for the pair, so two admins both clicking &quot;Yes, verify&quot; is the case that would produce two matches and two contradictory confirmation flows. The correct outcome is exactly one 200 (OK) and one 409 (Conflict). As in plan 13 the Response Assertion accepts either code per-sample because JMeter cannot compare across threads in one assertion; the real signal is the 1-and-1 split read off the Summary Report, and the database check below:

SELECT status FROM matching_service.match_appeals WHERE id = &apos;&lt;row&gt;&apos;;
-- must be exactly one decision: VERIFIED, decided_by set, decided_at set
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM matching_service.matches WHERE lost_item_id = &apos;&lt;row&gt;&apos; AND found_item_id = &apos;&lt;row&gt;&apos;;
-- must be exactly 1. Two rows here means both admins created a match: the DoD violation.

RACE_APPEAL_CSV holds exactly one PENDING appeal and is single-use per run. Rows are appealId,lostItemId,foundItemId; the two item ids are carried purely so the post-run match check above can be run without looking them up again. A row whose reports are not both still ACTIVE will fail with 409 for both threads (the endpoint refuses to verify against a missing report), which looks like a suspiciously clean race - check the status codes are 200/409 and not 409/409.

HOST/PORT default to localhost/5179. ADMIN_TOKEN is the JWT of a seeded admin (admin1@lostandfound.com).</stringProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="HOST" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">HOST</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(HOST,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(PORT,5179)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="ADMIN_TOKEN" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">ADMIN_TOKEN</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(ADMIN_TOKEN,SET_ME)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="RACE_APPEAL_CSV" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">RACE_APPEAL_CSV</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(RACE_APPEAL_CSV,storyLF338-admin-race-appeal.csv)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="Admin authentication + JSON content type">
        <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
          <elementProp name="auth-cookie" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Cookie</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">auth_token=${ADMIN_TOKEN}</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="content-type" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Sends the admin JWT as the auth_token cookie (MatchingService&apos;s JWT bearer handler accepts it as a fallback - ClaimRegistration.cs).</stringProp>
      </HeaderManager>
      <hashTree/>
      <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="Story LF-338 single race appeal" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Exactly one data row expected. shareMode &quot;All threads&quot; plus recycle=true means both threads read the SAME row, which is what makes them decide the same appeal rather than two different ones. Rows are appealId,lostItemId,foundItemId.</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="filename">${RACE_APPEAL_CSV}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="variableNames">appealId,lostItemId,foundItemId</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">false</boolProp>
        <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="quotedData">false</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
        <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
      </CSVDataSet>
      <hashTree/>
      <ThreadGroup guiclass="ThreadGroupGui" testclass="ThreadGroup" testname="Two simultaneous deciding admins" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Exactly 2 threads, 1 loop each, no ramp-up (both start together) - a fixed shape matching the Synchronizing Timer&apos;s hard-set group size of 2 below.</stringProp>
        <intProp name="ThreadGroup.num_threads">2</intProp>
        <intProp name="ThreadGroup.ramp_time">0</intProp>
        <boolProp name="ThreadGroup.same_user_on_next_iteration">true</boolProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="LoopController" guiclass="LoopControlPanel" testclass="LoopController">
          <stringProp name="LoopController.loops">1</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="LoopController.continue_forever">false</boolProp>
        </elementProp>
      </ThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="Verify the shared appeal" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">POST /api/admin/match-appeals/{id}/verify, fired by both threads at (as close to) the same instant as the Synchronizing Timer below can arrange. Verify is the irreversible branch - it creates the match row - so this is the race worth running. Exactly one of the two concurrent samples is expected to land 200 and the other 409; this sampler&apos;s own assertion only confirms each individual response was one of the two acceptable outcomes, not that the appeal actually split correctly.</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${HOST}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${PORT}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/api/admin/match-appeals/${appealId}/verify</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">POST</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">true</boolProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
          </elementProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <SyncTimer guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="SyncTimer" testname="Synchronizing Timer - hold both deciders until both are ready" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Blocks each thread here until all 2 threads have reached this point, then releases both together, so the two verify requests below fire as close to simultaneously as JMeter can manage. groupSize must stay 2 - it matches this plan&apos;s fixed 2-thread shape.</stringProp>
            <intProp name="groupSize">2</intProp>
            <longProp name="timeoutInMs">0</longProp>
          </SyncTimer>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="Verify returns 200 or 409" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="49586">200</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="49587">409</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Accepts either outcome per-sample. Two 200s here is the real bug this plan hunts - it would mean both admins created a match - and no single assertion can see it. Read the code column of the Summary Report and confirm one 200 and one 409, then run the two database checks in the plan description.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">40</intProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message"></stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
      </hashTree>
      <ResultCollector guiclass="ViewResultsFullVisualizer" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="View Results Tree">
        <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
        <objProp>
          <name>saveConfig</name>
          <value class="SampleSaveConfiguration">
            <time>true</time>
            <latency>true</latency>
            <timestamp>true</timestamp>
            <success>true</success>
            <label>true</label>
            <code>true</code>
            <message>true</message>
            <threadName>true</threadName>
            <dataType>true</dataType>
            <encoding>false</encoding>
            <assertions>true</assertions>
            <subresults>true</subresults>
            <responseData>false</responseData>
            <samplerData>false</samplerData>
            <xml>false</xml>
            <fieldNames>true</fieldNames>
            <responseHeaders>false</responseHeaders>
            <requestHeaders>false</requestHeaders>
            <responseDataOnError>false</responseDataOnError>
            <saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>true</saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>
            <assertionsResultsToSave>0</assertionsResultsToSave>
            <bytes>true</bytes>
            <sentBytes>true</sentBytes>
            <url>true</url>
            <threadCounts>true</threadCounts>
            <idleTime>true</idleTime>
            <connectTime>true</connectTime>
          </value>
        </objProp>
        <stringProp name="filename">../results/storyLF338-admin-race-tree.jtl</stringProp>
      </ResultCollector>
      <hashTree/>
      <ResultCollector guiclass="SummaryReport" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="Summary Report">
        <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
        <objProp>
          <name>saveConfig</name>
          <value class="SampleSaveConfiguration">
            <time>true</time>
            <latency>true</latency>
            <timestamp>true</timestamp>
            <success>true</success>
            <label>true</label>
            <code>true</code>
            <message>true</message>
            <threadName>true</threadName>
            <dataType>true</dataType>
            <encoding>false</encoding>
            <assertions>true</assertions>
            <subresults>true</subresults>
            <responseData>false</responseData>
            <samplerData>false</samplerData>
            <xml>false</xml>
            <fieldNames>true</fieldNames>
            <responseHeaders>false</responseHeaders>
            <requestHeaders>false</requestHeaders>
            <responseDataOnError>false</responseDataOnError>
            <saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>true</saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>
            <assertionsResultsToSave>0</assertionsResultsToSave>
            <bytes>true</bytes>
            <sentBytes>true</sentBytes>
            <url>true</url>
            <threadCounts>true</threadCounts>
            <idleTime>true</idleTime>
            <connectTime>true</connectTime>
          </value>
        </objProp>
        <stringProp name="filename">../results/storyLF338-admin-race-summary.jtl</stringProp>
      </ResultCollector>
      <hashTree/>
    </hashTree>
  </hashTree>
</jmeterTestPlan>